# The Steam UI's memory ceiling AND freeze while a game owns the screen (operator-locked plan, docs/WISPOS-NOTES.md "Steam UI memory: decisions", item 1; ENABLED BY DEFAULT in the
# release image, 0.1.0 plan decision): the UI web helpers live in a cgroup whose memory.high drops while a game runs and which is frozen while the UI is not in front, so the kernel
# pushes the hidden UI's cold pages to zram before it touches the game's and nothing touches them afterwards. It must be running BEFORE Steam starts: pages are charged to the cgroup
# that was current when they were allocated, so helpers have to be moved while young. The ceilings are the program's own defaults (os/reclaim/wisp-ui-reclaim.c, one place). It writes
# only cgroup control files (RAM) and signals nothing; when it stops (SIGTERM) it lifts the ceiling and thaws, so the UI is never left throttled. THAW RULE (docs/WISPOS-NOTES.md "The
# thaw rule"): the UI is thawed the moment sway reports a focus change to a window of class `steam`; no sway, a lost subscription or any doubt counts as "visible", never frozen blind.
#
# Why --freeze: the first real-game A/B (2026-10-11) showed that a ceiling ALONE makes the still-running UI thrash (Steam UI 84% of a core instead of 21%, Quake III 25.0 fps against
# 28.3); ceiling + freeze measured the quietest and most stable (27.7 fps, sd 0.06, UI CPU 7%) and is the only combination that is enabled. Not yet measured: an aged Steam session and a
# second game, and nothing of it has run from a release image on the handheld: it is the first thing the operator's flash exercises.
# OFF SWITCH without touching the read-only system: `touch /storage/.config/wispos/ui-reclaim-off` and restart (or `systemctl disable --now wisp-ui-reclaim` for the session).
# 2026-10-14 (operator decision, docs/WISPOS-NOTES.md "soft freeze"): the shipped policy is --follow-focus, NOT --freeze. The ceiling follows the focus exactly as before (idle ceiling with the
# UI in front, a lower one while a game is in front or starting) but nothing is ever frozen: stability and usability come first (a frozen UI cannot be reached from a game, and a helper crash
# while frozen left no Steam window and nothing to thaw), and wisp-ui-priority keeps the hidden UI quiet by scheduling priority instead. The game-time ceiling is 250 MiB (--high-game 262144000):
# the 96 MiB of the freeze policy makes a running, unfrozen UI thrash (measured 2026-10-13). The idle ceiling stays the program's default. Back to the old policy: change the argument to --freeze.
[Unit]
Description=WispOS Steam UI memory ceiling and freeze
Before=wisp-steam.service
After=sysinit.target
ConditionPathExists=!/storage/.config/wispos/ui-reclaim-off

[Service]
# the sway socket: the program follows which window is in front
Environment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/var/run/0-runtime-dir SWAYSOCK=/var/run/0-runtime-dir/sway-ipc.0.sock
ExecStart=/usr/bin/wisp-ui-reclaim --follow-focus --high-game 262144000
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
Nice=5

[Install]
WantedBy=wispos.target
